Status icons are addressed by NAME and resolved through the system icon
theme by the tray host itself. The package installs the default set into
the 'hicolor' fallback theme (see the hicolor/ folder):

  hicolor/22x22/status/yd-ind-idle.svg    - Yandex.Disk is synchronized
  hicolor/22x22/status/yd-ind-pause.svg   - daemon not started, paused or no network
  hicolor/22x22/status/yd-ind-error.svg   - an error occurs in synchronization
  hicolor/22x22/status/yd-busy[1-5].svg   - synchronization in progress

Icons yd-busy1..5 are shown in a loop (animated icon):

  /--> yd-busy1 --> yd-busy2 --> yd-busy3 ->\
 |                                           |
  \--------- yd-busy5 <-- yd-busy4 <--------/

The status SVGs are symbolic: they use the "current-color-scheme" style
classes (ColorScheme-Text / ColorScheme-NegativeText), so desktops like
KDE Plasma recolor them automatically to follow the light/dark palette.

hicolor/scalable/apps/yd-logo.svg is the application icon for launchers
and menus. dark/yd-logo.png is installed as /usr/share/yd-tools/icons/yd-logo.png
only to be embedded into notifications and dialogs, because GdkPixbuf has
no guaranteed SVG loader.

The dark/ and light/ folders keep the legacy icon sets (upstream 1.12.x);
they are not installed anymore.

NOTE: to change the icons shown in the tray, provide your own variants under
the same names with your icon theme (Context=Status), for example:
  /usr/share/icons/<theme>/22x22/status/yd-ind-idle.svg
  ~/.icons/<theme>/22x22/status/yd-busy1.svg
